  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing mean differences from a reference point (here, -500), which requires evaluating all outcomes relative to that point and weighting by probabilities. The individual does not mention any numerical comparison, nor differences from a reference value, nor a calculation involving probabilities and deviations. However, they do implicitly treat -500 as a reference (by contrasting it with zero and a larger loss), and they focus on the possibility of achieving an outcome better than the sure loss (i.e., 0 vs -500), which aligns with considering differences from a reference. While the qualitative idea of reference dependence is present, the systematic evaluation of all differences and their probabilities — a core part of the reason — is absent. The individual’s reasoning is more focused on the attractiveness of avoiding loss altogether (a 'gain' relative to reference) than on a balanced assessment of mean differences. Thus, while there is partial alignment in using a reference point, the full decision rule is not applied.",
